SCRIPTS = [
("01", "blue-bottle-zhang-yuan", "Blue Bottle Zhang Yuan Café", "Blue Bottle, jahng yooen", """\
The coffee here is made inside a shed. The shed is inside a house. And the house is inside what was once the most famous garden in Shanghai.

You're in Zhang Yuan, off North Maoming Road, in a lane of grey brick houses with heavy stone frames round their front doors. Those stone-framed doors are the reason this kind of house is called shikumen, which means, more or less, stone gate. The café is in one of them, at two-forty North Maoming Road. Step inside and you'll see a low, sloping roof of steel standing in the middle of an old room, quite separate from the walls around it.

Start with the garden, because there's no sign of it now. Zhang Yuan opened to the public in eighteen eighty-five, and for a while it was the place to be seen in the city. People called it the number one public garden of Shanghai. There were operas and boxing matches here, an early photographer's booth in the open air, and, the story goes, one of the first electric lamps ever lit in China. Later, the garden was built over with these lanes, and it became a neighbourhood. It's now the largest and best-preserved shikumen complex in the city, reopened in twenty twenty-two after a long restoration.

Now the café. It was designed by the Shanghai studio Neri and Hu, and the rules were strict. The old brick walls, doors, windows and light wells all had to stay exactly as they were. So the architects didn't touch them. They put a little steel hut in the middle of the room, under a steep roof, and that's where the coffee is made. They called it a primitive shelter.

Have a look at the old columns too. They've been fitted with metal rods and small ledges, so you can lean on them, perch on them, or rest a cup there.

And here's the thing. That's exactly what people living in these lanes always did: a shelf nailed to a wall, a bench pushed into an alley, a bit of borrowed space. The café isn't copying the architecture. It's copying the habits.

[beat]

When you've finished, wander on through the rest of Zhang Yuan's lanes. Walk slowly, and look at the carvings above the stone doorways as you go. No two are quite the same.
"""),

("02", "hotung-residence-shanghai", "Former Residence of Hotung 何东旧居", "Former Residence of ho tung", """\
Shanghai's grandest dictionary was written in a millionaire's living room.

You're on North Shaanxi Road, at number four-five-seven, near the corner with Beijing West Road. Behind the gate and the lawn there's a large, pale villa with a row of tall columns across its front. It sits right in the middle of its garden, which in this part of the city is a rare thing.

The man who built it was Sir Robert Hotung. He was a Eurasian businessman from Hong Kong who started out working for the trading house Jardine Matheson and ended up, by most accounts, the richest man in Hong Kong. He was knighted in nineteen fifteen. He bought this plot in nineteen twenty-six, when this road was called Seymour Road. The family moved in two years later, and his son Edward ran the family's Shanghai business from here.

Have a look at the front. It's built in the English manner, in brick, timber and stone. The tall columns hold up a covered hall, and that hall leads into a very large drawing room. The grounds once covered more than a hectare, and they were fenced with bamboo, so people walking past could catch glimpses of the house through the gaps.

After nineteen forty-nine the family went back to Hong Kong, and the house passed to the city. Then something rather unexpected happened. In nineteen fifty-seven, Mao Zedong suggested that the great Chinese dictionary, the Cihai, should be revised, and that Shanghai should do it. The next year, the editors moved in here. The new edition came out in nineteen seventy-nine, and the dictionary's publisher still works in this house today.

[beat]

So the house of one of the richest men in Asia became a house of words. Cihai, by the way, means "sea of words". Rather a good name for a dictionary, and for a building full of people trying to pin down a whole language.

Before you go, walk a few minutes south along North Shaanxi Road, past the plane trees, to number one-eight-six. That's Rong Zhai, the house built for the industrialist known as the flour king. It's another family villa, from nineteen eighteen, and it opens its doors for exhibitions.
"""),

("03", "jean-georges-mercato-three-on-the-bund", "Jean-Georges & Mercato", "zhahn zhorzh and mer kahto", """\
The best views of Shanghai's new skyline are from inside one of its oldest office blocks.

You're on the Bund, at Number Three, where Guangdong Road meets the river road. In front of you is a heavy stone building in the classical style, with a tall tower and cupola on its corner. That's Three on the Bund. Behind you, across the road and the riverside promenade, the towers of Pudong are on the far bank.

This was built in nineteen sixteen for the Union Assurance Company, in the grand classical style that every bank and insurer on this stretch was competing in. In two thousand and four, the American architect Michael Graves turned the inside into restaurants, shops and a gallery, and the French chef Jean-Georges Vongerichten opened his restaurant on the fourth floor. It's still there.

Here's the curious bit. One of the architects who worked on that renovation for Graves was a young man called Lyndon Neri. He went on to found the Shanghai practice Neri and Hu with his partner, Rossana Hu. In two thousand and sixteen, they were asked to redesign Jean-Georges. So the same architect came back to the same rooms, more than ten years later.

What they did was mostly take things away. Out went the heavy curtains and the dark wood. During the demolition, the builders found classical arched columns that had been hidden under later finishes. The architects left them bare, worn and all. They restored the old plaster mouldings, and they added antique mirrors etched with lines from French philosophers. If you go up, look for those.

Two floors higher, on the sixth, is Mercato, the chef's more relaxed Italian place, also by Neri and Hu. Mercato means market. It's sharing plates, wood and brick, and a noisier room.

[beat]

What I like about this building is that you're never allowed to forget where you are. Every new room in it has been arranged around the windows, and every window looks at the other side of the river.

When you come out, cross over to the riverside promenade and turn round. From there you can see the whole curve of the Bund, and pick out Number Three by its corner tower.
"""),

("04", "jesse-restaurant-tianping-road", "Jesse Restaurant", "Jesse Restaurant", """\
This is one of the most famous restaurants in Shanghai, and it's about the size of a large kitchen.

You're on Tianping Road, at number forty-one, in the old French Concession, just off Huaihai Road. The restaurant is in a narrow, ordinary lane house. There's a small shopfront and the name Jesse above the door. In Chinese it's Old Jesse, and if there's a queue on the pavement, you've definitely found it.

Inside, it's two cramped floors of tables pressed close together. Most guides agree it's been cooking here since two thousand and four, and this is the original. There are other branches round the city now, but most people will tell you this is the one.

What they cook is benbang, which is the local Shanghai home cooking. The phrase people use for it means, roughly, "thick oil and red sauce". It's sweet, glossy and dark with soy, and it's a long way from the light Cantonese food many visitors expect.

Three dishes come up again and again. First, the red-braised pork belly, cooked slowly in soy and sugar until it shines. Then the scallion fish head: a whole fish head buried under a heap of spring onions fried until they're sweet and almost black. It's the one people photograph. And third, rice cooked in a hot pot with crab meat and roe.

None of this is fancy, and that's the point. Shanghai is usually sold as a city of cocktail bars and towers. But what Shanghai people actually miss when they're away is food like this, the food their grandmothers made, served in a room where you can hear the next table's conversation.

[beat]

Don't be put off by the wait. Even with a booking, people often stand outside for a while. It's part of eating here.

Afterwards, walk north to the top of Tianping Road, where it meets Huaihai Road. Five roads come together there, and on the corner is the Wukang Mansion. It's a long, wedge-shaped block of flats from the nineteen twenties, shaped rather like the bow of a ship. It's at its best at dusk.
"""),

("05", "meet-the-bund-bfc", "Meet the Bund", "Meet the Bund", """\
Most of Shanghai's restaurants come from somewhere else in China. This one came from the south coast, from Xiamen, and it brought the whole province with it.

You're at the Bund Finance Center, on Zhongshan East Second Road, the newer, low-rise district at the southern end of the Bund, between the river and the old city. Meet the Bund is on the third floor of the South Block. Its Chinese name, Yu Waitan, means much the same thing.

It opened in twenty nineteen, set up by a Xiamen restaurant group called Yanyu, and it was the first serious Fujian restaurant in Shanghai. Two years later it won a Michelin star. A sister restaurant has since opened on the fifty-sixth floor of Raffles City, across Suzhou Creek, and the name now sits high on Asia's Fifty Best Restaurants list.

Fujian is the province facing Taiwan across the strait, and its cooking is very different from what you'll find here in Shanghai. It's lighter, built round seafood and slow, clear broths, and it uses a lot of red rice wine. The menu here is arranged by the dialects of Fujian, so you eat your way round the province one region at a time.

One dish worth looking for is Hetian chicken from Longyan, in the hills of western Fujian. It's steamed in a brine with rice wine, and it's simple and very good. The other is Fujian's most famous dish, Buddha Jumps Over the Wall. It's a rich soup of dried seafood, simmered for hours. The story goes that it smelled so good that a monk climbed over the monastery wall to get some.

[beat]

Some cuisines arrive in a city through immigrants and cheap noodle shops. Fujian's came to Shanghai the other way round, at the top, in a finance district, with a chef trained in Xiamen. That tells you something about who's eating in Shanghai now.

Before you leave, look for the Fosun Foundation building here at the Bund Finance Center. Its front is covered by a curtain of bronze-coloured tubes that slowly moves to and fro. It was designed by Thomas Heatherwick and Foster and Partners, and it's well worth waiting to see it move.
"""),

("06", "ops-cafe-taiyuan-road", "Ops Cafe", "Ops Cafe", """\
There's no espresso on this menu. No latte, no Americano. And you'll drink whatever you order standing up.

You're on Taiyuan Road, near the corner of Yongjia Road, in the leafy western part of the old French Concession. Look for a ground-floor shop in a residential building at Lane one-seven-seven. There's no big sign. It's about ten square metres. The easiest way to spot it is the queue.

Ops opened in August twenty seventeen. It was started by two baristas, Ahguang and Siwei, who are both serious figures in Chinese competitive coffee. Ahguang represented China at the World Barista Championship. The idea came from an iced coffee he drank in Thailand that tasted more like a cocktail than a coffee. So that's what they make: original drinks, built the way a bartender builds a drink, out of coffee plus fruit, tea, herbs, salt, or the odd splash of whisky.

The menu is tiny. There are four or five drinks at a time, and they change every season, so whatever you have today probably won't be here in a few months. One summer there was a drink made with coconut water and topped with a foam of salted macadamia and a little seaweed.

Have a look at how the queue works. Staff walk down the line taking your order and payment before you reach the door. At the counter, a barista mixes your drink in front of you and tells you how to drink it. Foam first, usually, and they'll suggest finishing it in about ten minutes, before it changes.

The name stands for three words: open-minded, possibilities, and space. That last one matters. There are no chairs, just a narrow ledge, so everyone stands shoulder to shoulder. The founders say that's intended. The crowding is meant to get you talking.

[beat]

So you queue for half an hour, drink something in ten minutes, and leave having chatted with a stranger. It's not very efficient, but that's what it's for.

Afterwards, take a stroll along Yongjia Road. It's one of the quietest streets in this part of the city, with old garden villas behind the plane trees and walls.
"""),

("07", "rockbund-art-museum", "Rockbund Art Museum 上海外滩美术馆", "Rockbund Art Museum", """\
This street used to be called Museum Road, and for once the name has come true again.

You're on Huqiu Road, at number twenty, a block back from the Bund, near where Suzhou Creek meets the Huangpu. In front of you is a compact Art Deco building of five storeys. That's the Rockbund Art Museum.

The story starts long before this building. In the eighteen seventies, the North China Branch of the Royal Asiatic Society, a learned society of scholars and amateurs, built a library, a lecture hall and a museum near here. It collected Chinese natural history specimens and objects, and it was one of the first modern museums in China. In eighteen eighty-six the city renamed this street Museum Road in its honour.

The building you're looking at replaced the old one in the early nineteen thirties. It was designed by George Wilson of the firm Palmer and Turner, the same practice behind the old HSBC bank building on the Bund. Look up at the front. It's Art Deco, but Chinese decorative elements have been worked into the design. Western architecture in Shanghai was starting to borrow from China rather than simply ignore it.

In two thousand and seven, the British architect David Chipperfield was brought in to restore it. He kept the front true to the original. Inside, he cleared the floors into plain, light galleries and cut a skylight through the top three storeys. He added a roof terrace that looks out over the river. The museum opened in twenty ten, as a non-profit space for contemporary art.

[beat]

It's worth noticing what happened here. Around this building, eleven old blocks were restored over seventeen years, and many became offices, shops and restaurants. This one went back to its first job, a place where the public come to look at things and argue about them.

Before you leave, walk round to the new museum square at the back and along Yuanmingyuan Road. It's one of the best-preserved streets of old office buildings in Shanghai. At the end of it, the iron Waibaidu Bridge crosses Suzhou Creek.
"""),

("08", "the-puli-hotel-shanghai", "The PuLi Hotel and Spa 璞丽酒店", "The poo lee Hotel and Spa", """\
The first thing this hotel asks you to do is slow down, which in Jing'an is quite a request.

You're on Changde Road, at the entrance to the PuLi, a tall, plain tower just off Yan'an Road. Behind it are the trees of Jing'an Park, and the hotel has been arranged to face them.

It opened in twenty oh nine as the first hotel from a small company that called its idea an "urban resort": something meant to feel like a retreat, in the middle of one of the busiest districts in Shanghai. The tower is twenty-six storeys tall, designed by the Japanese firm Kume Sekkei. The inside was done by the Indonesian studio Jaya and Associates. The brief said the park had to be part of the design, and you'll see that it is.

Go into the lobby and look for three things. First, the water: a long, shallow reflecting pool running beside the main public room, with rows of bamboo along the glass. Second, what everything is made of. The materials were chosen to be local: dark Chinese timber, cast bronze, air-dried clay tiles, and the grey brick you see in old Shanghai lane houses. Third, and this is the one people remember, in the library, above the fireplace, the panels are covered in stingray leather.

And the name. The pu in PuLi is the Chinese character for uncut jade, stone before anyone has polished it. It's a fair description of the building, too: nothing shiny, nothing gold.

[beat]

When this hotel opened, luxury in Shanghai meant chandeliers and marble. The PuLi went the other way. It went for quiet, grey brick, water and a view of trees. That approach has been copied all over the city since.

When you leave, cross into Jing'an Park itself. It's an old cemetery turned into a park, with some fine old plane trees. Keep going through it to Nanjing West Road and you'll come out opposite the golden roofs of Jing'an Temple.
"""),

("09", "the-shanghai-edition", "The Shanghai EDITION", "The Shanghai Edition", """\
For most of the last century, this building kept the lights on in Shanghai. Now the lights are the main attraction.

You're on Nanjing East Road, a few minutes' walk in from the Bund. In front of you is an Art Deco block in stone. It was the headquarters of the Shanghai Power Company, completed in nineteen twenty-nine. Rising behind it is a newer glass tower. Together they make up the Shanghai EDITION hotel.

The idea came from Ian Schrager, the New Yorker usually credited with inventing the boutique hotel. When the EDITION opened here in twenty eighteen, it kept the old power company building for the bars, restaurants, spa and rooftops. The hundred and forty-five bedrooms went into the new tower. The two halves are joined by what the hotel calls the Urban Lobby.

Go into the main lobby and look up. Hanging in the middle is a huge glowing sphere of light, made by the French designer Eric Schmitt. Around it, the grey stone walls rise nine and a half metres, set off with dark walnut. In a building that once ran the city's electricity supply, the centrepiece is a lamp, and I don't think that's an accident.

Then find the Urban Lobby. It has a hanging garden, an antique mirror seven storeys tall, and a bronze spiral staircase. That's how you get from the old building to the new one.

Upstairs is the Punch Room, a cocktail bar brought over from the EDITION in London. Right at the top is a roof garden looking out over the river to Pudong. It even has a lawn, and on some days, croquet.

[beat]

It would have been easy to keep the old building as a pretty face on the new tower. Instead, almost all the social life of the hotel happens inside the old walls. The tower is for sleeping. The power station is for going out.

When you come out, walk east to the end of Nanjing Road, where it meets the Bund. On the corner is the green pyramid roof of the Peace Hotel, the old Sassoon House. It's another Art Deco landmark, and it's two minutes away.
"""),

("10", "the-sukhothai-shanghai", "The Sukhothai Shanghai", "The sook oh tie Shanghai", """\
A Thai hotel, in Shanghai, designed as a garden by two Shanghai architects. It sounds like a muddle, but it works rather well.

You're on Weihai Road, at number three-eighty, at the hotel's entrance, in the middle of Jing'an. The old lanes of Zhang Yuan are a short walk away. Go in through the doors, and the lobby opens up in front of you.

The Sukhothai is a hotel name from Bangkok. When it came to Shanghai, it opened in twenty eighteen, and the brand asked the local studio Neri and Hu to design it. Their brief to themselves was an urban oasis. Every part of the building was planned as some kind of garden. The ground floor was treated as an open square. The upstairs corridors became garden paths. And each guest room was designed as a small house of its own.

Have a look at the staircase in the lobby. It's made of thick slabs of grey terrazzo. Each one is shifted slightly from the one below, so the stair seems to float as it climbs. It has a thin bronze handrail that looks barely attached.

Then look up. Above the stair hangs a huge wooden lantern, a woven lattice of timber. Inside it are dozens of bronze-and-glass globes. As you climb the stairs, you walk up through the lights.

Last, find the all-day restaurant and garden bar. The floors and walls are green terrazzo and tiles with a pattern of leaves, so you're in a garden even when it's raining.

[beat]

What I find interesting is that nothing here is directly Thai. There are no golden spires and no elephants. The architects took the spirit of the brand, which is calm and a closeness to nature, and translated it into Shanghai materials. It's a bit like a good translation of a poem: the same feeling, in another language.

When you leave, walk a few steps along Weihai Road to number four-twenty, the Cha House. It's a European-style mansion from the nineteen twenties that was moved and carefully restored. Then turn the corner into Zhang Yuan.
"""),

("11", "west-bund-museum", "West Bund Museum 西岸美術館", "West Bund Museum", """\
Not long ago, this stretch of riverbank was all coal yards, docks and an airfield. Now it's lined with art museums.

You're on Longteng Avenue, at the northern tip of a long riverside park, where the road meets the Huangpu. In front of you are three tall, pale blocks arranged around a lower centre, a bit like the blades of a pinwheel. That's the West Bund Museum, designed by the British architect David Chipperfield.

Have a look at what it's made of. The three blocks are clad in translucent glass, and the glass is recycled. Chipperfield's office describes its finish as opaline. In daylight it has a faint, milky shimmer, and after dark, when the galleries are lit, it glows. Between the blocks, the roof of the central lobby seems to hover, and it reaches out towards the river on two slim, tapering columns.

Now walk round to the river side. The whole building stands on a raised terrace, above the flood plain. Steps lead down to the water. There's a long, low café right on the edge.

The museum opened in November twenty nineteen, and it's best known for its partnership with the Centre Pompidou in Paris. The French president, Emmanuel Macron, came to the opening. Since then, works from the Pompidou's collection of modern art have been shown here in long-running exhibitions, and the partnership was renewed in twenty twenty-three for another five years.

[beat]

This was a city deciding what to do with its old industrial waterfront. Shanghai chose culture. In just a few years this riverbank has filled with museums, and many of them are in the old industrial buildings themselves.

From here, follow the riverside path upstream, along the water. You'll pass the Long Museum, built round an old concrete coal-unloading bridge. Further on is the Yuz Museum, in a former aircraft hangar from the old Longhua airport. It's a good walk, especially in the late afternoon.
"""),

("12", "xin-rong-ji-nanyang-road", "Xin Rong Ji", "shin rong jee", """\
This restaurant group started in a basement in a small coastal town. Its Shanghai flagship is in a mansion that belonged to the family of one of the world's most famous architects.

You're on Nanyang Road, at number one-seventy, a quiet street a few minutes' walk from Jing'an Temple. Behind the wall is a heritage villa with a garden. This is Xin Rong Ji's Shanghai flagship, and lately it's also been listed under the name Rong Fu Yan. There's no sign outside saying "come in". This is somewhere you book in advance.

Start with the house. It once belonged to the Pei family, an old Suzhou banking family. The architect I. M. Pei, who designed the glass pyramid at the Louvre, came from that family. Inside, the restaurant hangs some of Pei's own drawings. Look out too for decorative details in the style of Suzhou's gardens and houses.

Then there's the food. Xin Rong Ji was started in October nineteen ninety-five, in a basement in Linhai, in the Taizhou area of Zhejiang province, south of here on the coast, by a local man called Zhang Yong. It began as a seafood restaurant with a live tank. It still cooks Taizhou food: very fresh fish from the East China Sea, cooked simply so that the fish does the work.

The dish everyone talks about is yellow croaker, a delicate sea fish that has become rare and expensive in the wild. Here it's usually steamed or braised, and it's served with some ceremony.

The success has been remarkable. This restaurant won two Michelin stars in twenty eighteen. The next year, the group's Beijing restaurant became the first in mainland China to be awarded three stars.

[beat]

So a seafood restaurant from a provincial basement ended up in a banker's mansion, in the most expensive part of Shanghai. It got there by doing one simple thing, very well, for a very long time.

From here, walk east along Nanyang Road to Shaanxi North Road. Within a few minutes of that corner are two more great family houses: Rong Zhai at number one-eight-six, the flour king's villa, and the Hotung residence at number four-five-seven.
"""),

("13", "design-republic-design-commune", "设计共和 Design Republic", "Design Republic", """\
This building started life as a police station. Now people come here to buy chairs.

You're on Jiangning Road, at number five-eleven, in Jing'an. In front of you is a red-brick building with a band of plain glass along the street, where you'd expect a row of shopfronts. This is the Design Republic Design Commune. In Chinese, it's Sheji Gonghe.

The building went up in the nineteen-tens as a police headquarters, built by the British when this was part of the International Settlement. By the time the Shanghai architects Neri and Hu took it on, in twenty ten, it was almost derelict. The wood was rotten and the plaster was falling off.

The outside was protected, so most of it had to stay as it was. Look at the brick. The architects cleaned it, repaired it, and left it bare. Then look at the glass at street level. That's where a row of small shops once stood. They were removed and replaced with one long sheet of clear glazing. You can see straight through it to the old brick and the rough concrete inside.

Once inside, look for the cuts. Neri and Hu removed bits of floor, wall and ceiling in small, precise openings. Through them you can see old wooden laths and older brickwork, so you can read the building's history in layers, like rock in a cliff face.

Then the shop itself. Design Republic is a furniture shop that Lyndon Neri and Rossana Hu started themselves. When the building reopened in twenty twelve, it became their flagship, with a gallery, a café and a restaurant around it. It even has a small flat upstairs.

[beat]

So the architects took a building that had been about control, a police station, and opened it up. You can see in through the glass and through the walls. And the architects were also the shopkeepers, which is a nice way of making sure the building works.

If you've enjoyed their work, Neri and Hu's buildings are all over this city. The Blue Bottle café in Zhang Yuan and the lobby of the Sukhothai hotel on Weihai Road are both about a fifteen-minute walk south-east of here.
"""),
]
